rint before. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCambridge University Press & Assessment (https://assets.cambridge.org/97811075/64749/frontmatter/9781107564749_frontmatter.pdf)\nciteturn583search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Cambridge University Press & Assessment978-1-107-56474-9 — Indra's Pearls ... THE VISION OF FELIX KLEIN\nCambridge University Press & Assessment\n978-1-107-56474-9 — Indra's Pearls\nDavid Mumford, Caroline Series, David Wright\nFrontmatter\nMore Information\nINDRA’S PEARLS\nTHE VISION OF FELIX KLEIN\nwww.cambridge.org\n© in this web service Cambridge University Press & Assessment--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA delightful fiction (Chapter 2) - Indra's Pearls (https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/indras-pearls/delightful-fiction/E7D9CAB579E9A944FFDA0505F11B3752)\nciteturn583search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago;   * Preface ... This aura of mystery persisted well into the twentieth century – the senior author's aunt Margaret Silcock (née Mumford), who studied mathematics at Girton College Cambridge in 1916, liked to describe them as a ‘delightful fiction’. ... Indra's PearlsThe Vision of Felix Klein ...   * Book: Indra's Pearls\nBehind complex numbers is a wonderful synthesis between two dimensional geometry and an elegant arithmetic in which every polynomial equation has a solution. When complex numbers were first dreamed of in the Renaissance, they were treated as an esoteric, almost mystical, concept. This aura of mystery persisted well into the twentieth century – the senior author's aunt Margaret Silcock (née Mumford), who studied mathematics at Girton College Cambridge in 1916, liked to describe them as a ‘delightful fiction’. In fact we still use the term ‘imaginary numbers’ to this day. Modern scientists, however, take complex numbers for granted, FORTRAN makes them a predefined data type, and they are standard toolkit for any electronic engineer. Perhaps the most remarkable fact about complex numbers is that they are absolutely essential to modern physics. In the theory of quantum mechanics, not only can the universe exist probabilistically in two states at once, but the uncertain composite state is constructed by adding the two simple states together with complex coefficients, introducing a complex ‘phase’.\n\n* * *\n\n## Information\n\nType\n    Chapter\n\nInformation\n    \n\nIndra's Pearls\n\nThe Vision of Felix Klein\n\n, pp. 36 - 61\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781107050051.004 [Opens in a new window]\n\nPublisher: Cambridge University Press\n\nPrint publication year: 2002\n\n  * A delightful fiction\n  * David Mumford, Brown University, Rhode Island, Caroline Series, University of Warwick, David Wright, Oklahoma State University\n  * Book: Indra's Pearls\n  * Online publication: 05 January 2014\n  * Chapter DOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781107050051.004\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIndra's Pearls: The Vision of Felix Klein - David Mumford, Caroline Series, David Wright - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books/about/Indra_s_Pearls.html?hl=en&id=OzTzAgAAQBAJ&output=html_text)\nciteturn583search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 24.5 years ago; Crawled: last week; # Indra's Pearls: The Vision of Felix KleinDavid Mumford, Caroline Series, David Wright Cambridge University Press, Apr 25, 2002 - Mathematics - 416 pages Felix Klein, one of the great nineteenth-century geometers, discovered in mathematics an idea prefigured in Buddhist mythology: the heaven of Indra contained a net of pearls, each of which was reflected in its neighbour, so that the whole Universe was mirrored in each pearl. ... abAB algebra algorithm angle Apollonian gasket baBA blue boundary calculate called centre chain Chapter coloured complex numbers conjugate corresponding curve cusp groups cyclic permutations 