d infinitely repeated reflections and was led to forms with multiple coexisting symmetries. For a century, these images barely existed outside the imagination of mathematicians. However, in the 1980s, the authors embarked on the first computer exploration of Klein's vision, and in doing so found many further extraordinary images. Join the authors on the path from basic mathematical ideas to the simple algorithms that create the delicate fractal filigrees, most of which have never appeared in print before. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIndra's Pearls | CiNii Research (https://cir.nii.ac.jp/crid/1362262944110311936)\nciteturn583search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 24.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago;   * The Vision of Felix Klein ...   * https://www.cambridge.org/core/terms ... <jats:p>Felix Klein, one of the great nineteenth-century geometers, discovered in mathematics an idea prefigured in Buddhist mythology: the heaven of Indra contained a net of pearls, each of which was reflected in its neighbour, so that the whole Universe was mirrored in each pearl.\n\n# Indra's Pearls\n\nDOI 被引用文献6件\n\n  * David Mumford\n    \n    \n\n  * Caroline Series\n    \n    \n\n  * David Wright\n    \n    \n\n## 書誌事項\n\nタイトル別名\n    \n\n  * The Vision of Felix Klein\n\n公開日\n    2002-04-25\n\n権利情報\n    \n\n  * https://www.cambridge.org/core/terms\n\nDOI\n    \n\n  * 10.1017/cbo9781107050051\n\n公開者\n    Cambridge University Press\n\n## 説明\n\n<jats:p>Felix Klein, one of the great nineteenth-century geometers, discovered in mathematics an idea prefigured in Buddhist mythology: the heaven of Indra contained a net of pearls, each of which was reflected in its neighbour, so that the whole Universe was mirrored in each pearl. Klein studied infinitely repeated reflections and was led to forms with multiple coexisting symmetries. For a century, these images barely existed outside the imagination of mathematicians. However, in the 1980s, the authors embarked on the first computer exploration of Klein's vision, and in doing so found many further extraordinary images. Join the authors on the path from basic mathematical ideas to the simple algorithms that create the delicate fractal filigrees, most of which have never appeared in print before. Beginners can follow the step-by-step instructions for writing programs that generate the images. Others can see how the images relate to ideas at the forefront of research.</jats:p>\n\n## 詳細情報 詳細情報について\n\n  * CRID\n    1362262944110311936\n  * DOI\n    10.1017/cbo9781107050051\n  * データソース種別\n    \n    * Crossref\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIndra's pearls : the vision of Felix Klein | WorldCat.org (https://search.worldcat.org/title/Indra%27s-pearls-%3A-the-vision-of-Felix-Klein/oclc/49859120)\nciteturn583search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 24.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; Image: Front cover image for Indra's pearls : the vision of Felix Klein ... Summary:\"Felix Klein, one of the great nineteenth-century geometers, discovered in mathematics an idea prefigured in Buddhist mythology: the heaven of Indra contained a net of pearls, each of which was reflected in its neighbour, so that the whole Universe was mirrored in each pearl. ... Print Book, English, 2002\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFelix Klein (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Felix_Klein)\nciteturn583search15 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; * David Mumford, Caroline Series, and David Wright Indra's Pearls: The Vision of Felix Klein.Cambridge Univ. ... He supervised the first Ph.D. thesis in mathematics written at Göttingen by a woman, by Grace Chisholm Young, an English student of Arthur Cayley's, whom Klein admired. ... It was Lie who introduced Klein to the concept of group, which was to have a major role in his later work. ... - :de:Werner Burau and :de:Bruno Schoeneberg \"Klein, Christian Felix